<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Racial segregation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Racial segregation is the enforced separation of people of different races in housing, public life and rights. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> codified racial segregation in its African colonies through a series of laws, particularly after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. These measures were designed to preserve Italian dominance over the colonised populations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/racial-segregation.jpg" alt="Racial segregation" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esther Bubley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1943_Colored_Waiting_Room_Sign.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The administration introduced <b>racial segregation</b>, codified from 1937 in laws forbidding marriage and concubinage between Italians and Africans, to preserve Italian dominance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of South Africa moved from the most elaborate system of legal <b>racial segregation</b> of the twentieth century to a negotiated multiracial democracy in 1994, and as one of Africa’s largest economies and a leading voice of the Global South it remains central to the continent’s politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Racial segregation</b> in South Africa long predated apartheid.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the doctrine of states’ rights was invoked to defend slavery and then the <b>racial segregation</b> of the southern states, and federal troops had to enforce the Supreme Court’s ruling against segregated schools in Brown v. Board of Education of 1954, as at Little Rock,…</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>…come to power in 1948 and governed for the next 46 years; under apartheid, a brutal system of <b>racial segregation</b> whose name meant &quot;separateness&quot; in Afrikaans, the white minority held all political power and black South Africans were forced to live in poverty-stricken townships and denied basic human…</blockquote><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands</a></li><li><blockquote>The US Supreme Court ruled that <b>racial segregation</b> on Louisiana railways did not violate the Constitution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
